  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry conditions persist across eastern New York and western New\nEngland as a surface anticyclone remains anchored over the Mid-\nAtlantic and high pressure ridging continues to build overhead.\nAtmospheric heights on the order of 1.5 to nearly 2.5 STDEVs\nabove normal through Sunday will maintain above-normal\ntemperatures largely in the 50s with valley areas seeing low 60s\ntoday and tomorrow. Winds will continue to be light through this\nperiod as well, though a light breeze will kick up out of the\nnorthwest tomorrow with the passing of a weak upper-level\nshortwave. In other words, it's going to continue to be\nabsolutely beautiful through Sunday. Enjoy! \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMedium-range guidance continues to resolve the development of a\ncoastal low pressure system deep in the southwest Atlantic on\nFriday, which subsequently tracks northward along the East Coast\nthroughout the weekend into Monday. While this system holds our\nnext best chance for appreciable rainfall, there continues to be\na considerable amount of uncertainty pertaining to the track of\nthis storm which will ultimately determine our local impacts.\nRun-to-run model simulations and ensembles have largely been \nalternating between two primary solutions: one which brings \nwidespread rainfall with heavy amounts in portions of the Mid-\nHudson Valley and southwest New England, and one wherein merely \nmeasurable rain falls across areas within, south, and east of \nthe Capital District. \n\nIn the event of the first, a weakening cutoff low in the\nnorthern Plains would devolve into a shortwave secondary to a\ndeepening primary shortwave just to the west of the Hudson Bay.\nThe deepening of the primary wave would consequently trigger the\nslowing and slight deepening the secondary over the western\nGreat Lakes. This would pull the coastal low farther inland and\nbring us more rainfall as a favorable jet structure increases\nour potential at a predecessor rain event (PRE). In the second\nscenario, the weaker shortwave doesn't phase with the primary,\nallowing it to maintain its strength and even weaken on its\nprogressive track. This would lead to the coastal low being\nforced farther eastward and shift the axis of heaviest rainfall\nto our east. According to latest guidance, this second scenario\nhas become the more common one. However, we will continue to\nclosely monitor the trends of this system over the coming days\nin order to better iron out the details.",
